////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Negative tests for the Peggy parsers
//
// Every line in this file is expected to FAIL to parse in BOTH the
// putdown and tex parsers (with default options), locking in the
// rejection of invalid syntax.  This file is only used by the
// golden-snapshot suite in parsertests.js, NOT by the .parsertest
// command in Lode, which requires its files to parse successfully.

// negation cannot apply directly to another negation without parentheses
not not P
// ticks bind too loosely to allow application of a complemented function
f'(x)
// exponentiation requires parentheses to disambiguate towers
x^y^z
// in default (non-set) mode curly braces are environments, not sets
2 in {1,2,3}
// incomplete expressions
x +
(x
x^
f(
{ x
forall x
Rule {
{ :P
// malformed numbers
0.5.2
x..y
// the index bracket notation is strictly binary
[G:H:K]
// exponents other than atomics require parentheses, e.g. 2^([G:H])
2^[G:H]
// the EFA marker is @ or 𝜆 (U+1D706); Greek λ (U+03BB) is rejected
λP(k)
// classified keywords are not symbols (the token classification is
// the authority: 'sum' is an operator name, 'of' and 'for' are
// structural keywords - while 'as' is no keyword at all, just an
// ordinary symbol, and lives in LurchParserTests)
sum
of + 1
for + 1
// the 'is a <noun> <prep>' registry: a committed noun row
// with an unparseable right-hand side does not fall back past the is row,
// whose 'subset' operand is a classified operator word; a wrong article
// never matches the noun row at all; and a phrase leftover that cannot
// reparse ('is A' dangling after the right-hand hole) is an error, not a
// junk LC sequence
x is a subset of, P
A is an subset of B
x is a element of A
~ is not a relation on x is A
// naked bindings are not relation arguments: a binding operand
// must be parenthesized to keep its scope legible - (x.f(x)) ~ (y.f(y))
// parses via the ordinary paren operand.  (The | and bare-cong
// versions of these locks live in LurchParserTests: a chain step's
// right-hand side accepts a
// Binding, so 'a | x.y' parses like
// 'a = x.y'.)
a in x.y
x is x.y
a ~ x.y
a rel x.y
a ≈ x.y
a ~ x mapsto y
A is a partition of x.y
A is not a partition of x.y
// 'normal' is an operator word now (the ⊴ row), so it cannot be an
// ordinary symbol in operand position
normal + 1
// a relation token whose leftover cannot reparse as a symbol ('in' is a
// reserved word), and mixed relation families that do not chain
// ('a subset b = c' and 'a = b subset c' are ⊆-family transitive
// chains, locked in LurchParserTests)
x in
a in b in c
// committed keyword choices with unparseable right-hand sides where the
// leftovers cannot reparse as symbols either
x is an and
x is not and y is b
x is a partition of, P
// an ended expression whose leftover cannot reparse.  An indexed
// the star family shares one level, so the leftover '⊙ c' (raw ⊙ has no
// symbol rendering) fails; and '+ b' is not a valid operand for the first
// '+'.  ('Union(B,k,A) + x' parses: delimited call forms are
// closed units - locked in LurchParserTests.)
a ⊗ b ⊙ c
a + + b
// interior big-operator keywords (of,
// for, in, to, =, d) are case-sensitive lowercase for every operator,
// and the English ones require whitespace on both sides - `in(S)` reads
// as applying a function named `in`, since function application forbids
// a space before its parentheses
sum OF f for k in S
sum f FOR k in S
Union OF B for k in S
Union B FOR k in S
sum k=1 TO n of f
sum k=1 to n OF f
sum k in(S) of f
sum f for k in(S)
Union k in(S) of B
// the same no-abutting-paren rule covers every alphabetic keyword:
// a ¬-desugar word or an interior tail keyword before '('
// has no application fallback, so these are errors, not splits
a notin(b)
a neq(b)
f : A to(B)
// the retired variant zoo: the from/as/goes limit forms and the wrt
// indefinite forms no longer parse
sum k from 0 to n of k^2
sum of k^2 as k goes from 0 to n
sum k^2 for k to n
sum of k^2 for k to n
int x from 0 to n of x^2
int of x^2 as x goes from 0 to n
int x^2 for x to n
int with respect to x of x^2
int wrt x of x^2
int of x^2 with respect to x
// the limit form requires its 'of' ('sum k to 5 k^3' is awkward to read)
sum k to n f
sum k to 5 k^3
// defint is only the Algebrite call form
defint k = 1 to n of f
// every Union/Intersect name is strictly
// case-sensitive: an off-case bigcup is an ordinary symbol, so the
// English form's 'of' keyword is a leftover reserved word
BIGCUP k in NN of A(k)
Bigcap k in NN of A(k)
// maps holes are plain relation arguments - no bindings, and no chaining
// of the 'to' keyword
f : A to x.y
f : A to B to C

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Token classification, the mention rule, and the keyword-case
// sweep.  A classified word in
// operand position is a parse error (with a hint naming the mention
// syntax) instead of silently parsing as a symbol, and
// junk-parses that dangle leftover operator words as separate symbol
// LCs are errors too.

// structural keywords are not symbols anywhere
1+by^2
1+assume^2
Comment
x + and
// phrase replacement requires word boundaries on both sides:
// a missing space after 'for all' is flagged, not guessed away
for allz.T(z)
// the negated English verbs keep the verb stem (no s)
a does not loves b
// operator words and glyphs are not symbols in unmarked operand
// positions (mention them as (op) or 'op' instead)
lt
x^star
star = y
// a mistyped big-operator call form stays an error instead of silently
// degrading to a function application (bare application of a
// big-operator name is excluded from the head mention hole)
sum(f,k)
defint(f,x)
int x^2 wrt x
// relations consume exactly one step; a dangling relation keyword can
// no longer reparse as a symbol LC, so the whole input fails
a is b is c
a sim b sim c
a ~ b subset c
a cong b mod c cong d mod e
// the single-step relation rows (⊑ ⊴ ~ ≈ rel) do not chain: an
// n-ary run like 'a ~ b ~ c' is a parse error, because the
// leftover ' ~ c' has no LC reading (~ is not a chain operator)
x~y~z
x ~y~ z
x ~ y ~ z
a ~ b ~ c
a~(b+1)~c~d^2
H ⊑ K ⊑ G
H subgroup G sqsubseteq K
A normal B ⊴ C
a rel b rel c
a ≈ b ≈ c
// cross-family chain tokens end the chain, and these leftovers have no
// LC reading (a lone | / subset / < cannot start an LC)
a subset b | c
a | b subset c
a subset b lt c
a < b subset c
a cong b | c
a cong_(m) b cong c
// a committed keyword choice ('is a') whose right-hand side fails has no
// fallback to bare 'is', and the leftovers are no longer symbols
x is a and y is b
x is a, y is b
x is the and y
// ditto at end of input: 'is a' commits and
// the missing right-hand side is an error, like the lines above
a is a
// a same-level climb token that cannot continue its run ends the
// expression (choose is not chainable; the star family shares a level);
// the leftover operator no longer reparses, so these old three-LC
// junk-parses are errors now
a choose b choose c
a choose b + c choose d
a star b oplus c
a star b oplus c + d
a choose b + c choose d + e
a oplus - b
// iff is non-associative (assoc 'none'): an unparenthesized
// iff run ends before its second iff, whose leftover is a targeted error
// (neither nesting is the pairwise chain a reader means by 'P iff Q iff
// R' - parenthesize)
P iff Q iff R
P iff Q implies R iff S
// a parameterized star token cannot continue a run whose parameters
// differ or are absent: the leftover '⊕ c' has no LC reading (the
// equal-parameters case parses left-nested, and the mismatched-params
// case junk-splits via the subscripted mention - both locked in
// LurchParserTests)
a ⊕_(n) b ⊕ c
// exponentiation still binds above application: an application result
// is not an exponent base (write (f(x))^2 or f^2(x) -
// this and the exponent-tower lock above get instructive targeted
// messages, see enrichParseError), the @P(k) EFA form is an application
// too, and a compound exponent still wants parentheses (write
// 2^(sum(f,k,1,n)))
f(x)^2
@P(k)^2
f^2(x)^2
2^sum(f,k,1,n)
// dangling operators after a completed relation no longer parse as junk
A subset B subset
x neq
a ~
// congruence chains: a chain committed to the congruence family
// does not consume an inequality step - the chain ends before the
// cross-family token, which has no operand reading, so the whole input
// is a targeted error
a cong_(m) b < c
a cong_(m) b leq c
x = y cong_(m) z < w
// ('a cong b' is NOT an error: bare congruence is a chain
// family - the geometer's (≅ a b) - locked in LurchParserTests,
// along with the 'a cong b MOD m' / 'a cong mod b TO c' keyword-case
// locks, which junk-parse via bare cong exactly as their = versions do)
// the keyword-case sweep: the interior keywords (mod, the
// quantifier/Let/ForSome 'in', be such that, and the is-phrase nouns)
// are case-sensitive lowercase.  (Uppercase IN/BE/SUCH/THAT remain ordinary
// symbols, so 'Let x IN A' still parses - as a bare Let plus symbol LCs;
// those junk-parse locks live in LurchParserTests.)
a cong MOD b to c
forall x IN S. P
// `a cong mod m to b` is not a congruence surface: the bare-cong
// chain grabs 'mod' as its
// right-hand operand and the leftover 'm to b' has no LC reading ('to'
// is reserved), so the whole input is an error
a cong mod m to b
X is a Partition of Y
// sequents: `not` binds a PropArg, never a bare sequent - negating one
// requires the parenthesized form, not (⊢ b) - and the turnstile words
// are classified operator words, not symbols.  (`a ⊢ b ⊢ c` is NOT an
// error: it is the sequent a ⊢ b followed by the prefix sequent ⊢ c,
// locked in LurchParserSequentTests)
not ⊢ b
not vdash b
// commas inside plain parentheses still only mean a sequent's sides: no
// turnstile, no reading (comma-separated sides live in ParenSequent
// only, so `(a, b)` stays the parse error it always was)
(a, b)
// a sequent needs at least one conclusion
(a, b ⊢)
⊢
// a multi-step transitive chain asserts several claims at once, so it
// cannot be a single sequent premise or conclusion (targeted error)
x = y = z ⊢ P
(x = y = z ⊢ P)
⊢ x = y = z
